<section aria-labelledby="defaults"><h2 id="defaults">The defaults</h2>
<p>A new application frames with <code>LengthDelimitedFrameCodec</code>: a 4-byte big-endian length prefix, then the payload, with frames over 1,024 bytes refused. Everything inside the frame is bincode, and the connection loop wakes every 100&nbsp;ms to clear stale partial messages.</p>
<p><code>buffer_capacity</code> raises or lowers the frame limit for the length-delimited codec. <code>codec()</code> returns the installed codec, which is how the sample checks the change took effect:</p>
{{ code.code('tests/codec.rs', 'defaults', title='codec.rs · the defaults') }}
{% call ui.callout('note', 'Changing the codec resets what depends on it') %}<p><code>buffer_capacity</code> and <code>with_codec</code> both clear any fragmentation settings, because the fragment size depends on the frame limit, and <code>with_codec</code> also removes hooks installed with <code>with_protocol</code>. Call <code>with_codec</code> first, then <code>enable_fragmentation()</code> and <code>with_protocol</code>.</p>{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="framecodec"><h2 id="framecodec">Write a FrameCodec</h2>
<p>A protocol with its own framing implements <code>FrameCodec</code>. The trait owns a Tokio <code>Decoder</code> and <code>Encoder</code> for its frame type and tells wireframe four things about a frame: where its payload is, how to wrap an outgoing payload, where a correlation ID lives if the header carries one, and how large a frame may be.</p>
<p>The example below is a small invented protocol: two bytes of length, four bytes of tag, then the payload. The decoder waits until a whole frame has arrived, refuses one that exceeds the limit, and splits the payload off without copying it.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/codec.rs', 'codec-types', title='codec.rs · frame, decoder, encoder') }}
{{ code.code('tests/codec.rs', 'codec-impl', title='codec.rs · the FrameCodec') }}
<p><code>frame_payload_bytes</code> is optional. Its default copies the payload slice; overriding it to clone a <code>Bytes</code> hands the payload on with a reference-count increment instead. <code>correlation_id</code> is consulted only when the decoded envelope has none of its own.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="install"><h2 id="install">Install it</h2>
<p><code>with_codec</code> swaps the codec and changes the application&rsquo;s type. wireframe clones the codec for each connection, so a codec that keeps state, such as a sequence counter, should make sure a clone starts fresh.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/codec.rs', 'install', title='codec.rs · with_codec') }}
<p>The sample then feeds an encoded request through the testkit one byte at a time. The decoder returns nothing until a whole frame has arrived, and the reply comes back intact.</p>
<p>wireframe ships three worked codecs in its repository, for Hotline, MySQL, and the Redis serialization protocol (RESP). They are listed on <a href="/wireframe/examples/">the examples page</a>. The Hotline codec is also reachable as <code>wireframe::codec::examples::HotlineFrameCodec</code>, though that module is hidden from the API reference.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="preamble"><h2 id="preamble">Read a preamble first</h2>
<p>Some protocols open with a handshake before any frame: a magic number, a version, a client name. The server reads it with <code>with_preamble::&lt;T&gt;()</code>, where <code>T</code> is any bincode type, decoding at most 1&nbsp;KiB with big-endian, fixed-width integers. Bytes read past the preamble are replayed into the framing layer, so nothing is lost.</p>
<p>The read always has a deadline: 10 seconds by default. <code>preamble_timeout</code> replaces it, <code>disable_preamble_timeout</code> removes it, and <code>Duration::ZERO</code> clamps to 1&nbsp;ms. Even without <code>with_preamble</code> the server reads a first byte under this deadline before it runs the application, so it never speaks first and a client that waits silently is disconnected. Call <code>with_preamble</code> before registering preamble hooks, because it clears any set earlier.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/preamble.rs', 'hello', title='preamble.rs · the handshake') }}
{{ code.code('tests/preamble.rs', 'server', title='preamble.rs · the server') }}
<p>The success handler receives the decoded preamble and the raw TCP stream, so it can reply before framing starts. The handler above always answers, then refuses an unknown magic number by returning an error. The client sends its preamble with <code>with_preamble</code> as soon as it connects:</p>
{{ code.code('tests/preamble.rs', 'client', title='preamble.rs · the client') }}
{% call ui.callout('fact', 'An error from the success handler closes the connection') %}<p>The server writes nothing on the application&rsquo;s behalf, so write any rejection before returning the error, and do not return one for a condition that is not fatal. A preamble that fails to decode, or misses the deadline, goes to <code>on_preamble_decode_failure</code> instead, and the connection closes after it runs.</p>{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="errors"><h2 id="errors">Codec errors</h2>
<p>In {{ wireframe_version }} the connection loop treats a decoder error as fatal: the connection closes. For the default codec that includes a frame over the limit. A frame that decodes but whose payload is not a valid envelope is different: it counts towards the limit of ten deserialization failures described in <a href="/wireframe/guides/routing/#unrouted">the routing guide</a>. The bundled bincode decoders also limit the work a peer can demand with a declared length: a fixed 16&nbsp;MiB for messages and 1&nbsp;KiB for preambles. That is separate from the frame limit, and custom decoders need their own validation.</p>
{% call ui.callout('fact', 'Recovery policies are defined but not consulted') %}<p>The <code>wireframe::codec</code> module defines a richer taxonomy (<code>CodecError</code>, with framing, protocol, I/O, and end-of-stream variants), a <code>RecoveryPolicy</code> of drop, quarantine, or disconnect, a <code>RecoveryPolicyHook</code> trait, and an <code>on_eof</code> protocol hook. The {{ wireframe_version }} server does not call any of them, so implementing one changes nothing yet. Tracking: <a href="https://github.com/leynos/wireframe/issues/878">#878</a> and <a href="https://github.com/leynos/wireframe/issues/879">#879</a>.</p>{% endcall %}
</section>
{% endblock %}
